course: dictionary
lesson: 12739
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The word drove has three jobs, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Drove is the past of drive: «Yesterday Mum drove us to the beach.» It means she steered the car, and it already happened.
explain: A drove is a group of animals moving together, like cattle or sheep: «A drove of cows walked slowly down the lane.» Here drove is a thing you can count, so it is a noun. Check the clues: animals walking together means a drove.
explain: «In droves» means in very large numbers: «Fans came in droves to the football match.» Try swapping in "in very large numbers" and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have found the meaning.
fact: Drive is the basic word. Its forms are drives, drove, driven and driving, and drove is the past form.
fact: «She drove to school this morning.» Drove tells us the driving already happened.
fact: A drove is a group of animals, such as cattle or sheep, moving together.
fact: «In droves» means in very large numbers.
fact: In history, drovers moved droves of cattle along roads to market.
exercise: «Yesterday, Mum drove us to the swimming pool.» What does drove mean here? | *Steered a car and took us somewhere | A group of animals walking together | A kind of boat | In very large numbers
exercise: «A drove of sheep ran across the field.» What is a drove in this sentence? | A fast car on the road | A ticket for the fair | *A group of animals moving together | A big hill covered in grass
exercise: «Shoppers came in droves on the last day of the sale.» What does in droves mean? | *In very large numbers | Carrying a map | Angrily, with shouting | Very slowly
test: Maths: «The bus drove 120 kilometres in two hours.» How far did it go in one hour, if it went the same speed each hour? | *60 kilometres | 118 kilometres | 240 kilometres | 122 kilometres
test: History: «Long ago, drovers moved droves of cattle to market.» What did a drover do? | *Led herds of animals along the road to market | Built wooden boats for the river | Sold tickets at the fair | Carried letters between towns
exam: Which word is the past form of drive? | Drives | Driving | *Drove | Driven
